REST API Design

1. What is REST?
    REST = Representational State Transfer.
    A REST API exposes resources through URLs and uses standard HTTP methods to operate on those resources.

    Example resource:
    users:
        API:
            GET    /users
            GET    /users/123
            POST   /users
            PUT    /users/123
            PATCH  /users/123
            DELETE /users/123

    Think:
        Resource = User
                ↓
        HTTP method + URL
                ↓
        Operation


2. HTTP Methods
    GET — retrieve
        GET /users
        Get all users.

    GET /users/123
        Get one user.

    POST — create
        POST /users
        {
            "name": "John",
            "email": "john@example.com"
        }

        Creates a new user.

    PUT — replace
        PUT /users/123
        Typically means replacing the resource representation.

        {
            "name": "John",
            "email": "new@example.com",
            "age": 30
        }

        Think:
            PUT = replace/update the whole resource

    PATCH — partial update
        PATCH /users/123
        {
            "age": 31
        }

        Only modifies the specified fields.

        Think:
            PATCH = partial update

    DELETE
        DELETE /users/123
        Deletes the resource.


3. PUT vs PATCH — Interview Favorite
    PUT
        Existing:
            {
            name: "John",
            age: 30
            }

        PUT:
            {
            name: "Alex",
            age: 25
            }
    Conceptually replaces the representation.
    
    PATCH
        PATCH:
            {
            age: 25
            }
    Only updates age.

    Interview answer:
        "PUT is generally used to replace a resource representation, while PATCH is used for partial modifications."

    Don't claim that every framework strictly enforces replacement semantics; HTTP semantics and application implementation are separate.


4. HTTP Status Codes
    2xx — Success
        200 OK
        Successful request.

    GET /users/123
        → 200
            201 Created
        Resource successfully created.

    POST /users
        → 201
            204 No Content
        Successful request with no response body.

    Common example:
        DELETE /users/123
        → 204   


5. 4xx — Client errors
    400 Bad Request
        Invalid request.
        {
            "email": "not-an-email"
        }

    401 Unauthorized
        Authentication is missing/invalid.
        Example:
            No valid JWT
        Important distinction:
            401 = authentication problem

    403 Forbidden
        User is authenticated but doesn't have permission.
            Authenticated admin → allowed
            Authenticated normal user → forbidden
            403 = authorization problem

    404 Not Found
        Resource doesn't exist.
        GET /users/999999

    409 Conflict
        Request conflicts with current resource state.
        Example:
            POST /users

        with an email that must be unique and already exists.

    422 Unprocessable Content
        Request is syntactically valid but fails semantic validation.
        For example:
            {
                "age": -10
            }

    Whether your API uses 400 or 422 for validation failures is a design choice; be consistent.


6. 5xx — Server errors
    500 Internal Server Error
        Unexpected server-side failure.

    502 Bad Gateway
        A gateway/proxy received an invalid response from an upstream service.

    503 Service Unavailable
        Service temporarily unavailable.


7. REST URL design
    Bad:
        GET /getUsers
        POST /createUser
        POST /deleteUser

    Better:
        GET    /users
        POST   /users
        DELETE /users/123

    The HTTP method should communicate the operation.


8. Nested resources
    Suppose users have orders.
    You could have:
        GET /users/123/orders
        Get orders belonging to user 123.
    And:
        GET /users/123/orders/456
        Get order 456 belonging to user 123.

    But don't over-nest URLs unnecessarily.
    Avoid something like:
        /users/123/orders/456/items/789/reviews/111/comments/222

    That becomes difficult to maintain.


9. Query parameters
    Query parameters are useful for:
        filtering
        sorting
        pagination
        searching

    Example:
        GET /users?role=admin
            Filtering.

        GET /users?sort=name
            Sorting.

        GET /users?page=2&limit=20
            Pagination.


10. Pagination
    Never blindly return:
        GET /users
        with millions of records.

    Basic pagination:
        GET /users?page=2&limit=20

    Response:
        {
            "data": [...],
            "page": 2,
            "limit": 20,
            "total": 500
        }

    For very large datasets, cursor-based pagination can be preferable.
    Example:
        GET /users?limit=20&cursor=abc123


11. Offset vs Cursor Pagination
    Offset
        ?page=10&limit=20

        Database conceptually does:
            skip 180
            take 20

        Simple, but deep offsets can become inefficient and results can shift when data changes.
    
    Cursor
        ?limit=20&cursor=abc123
        The cursor represents a position in the dataset.

        Often better for:
            large datasets
            infinite scrolling
            frequently changing data
            Interview answer

    "Offset pagination is simpler and works well for many use cases, while cursor-based pagination is generally better for large or frequently changing datasets because it avoids increasingly expensive offsets and provides more stable traversal."


12. Filtering + Sorting + Pagination
    A realistic API:
        GET /products
            ?category=mobile
            &minPrice=10000
            &maxPrice=50000
            &sort=price
            &page=2
            &limit=20

    This is much more realistic than just knowing:
        GET /users


13. API versioning
    Suppose you release:
        /api/v1/users

    Later you introduce breaking changes:
        /api/v2/users

    Versioning helps clients migrate without immediately breaking existing consumers.
    Other approaches exist, such as header/media-type versioning, 
    but URL versioning is common and easy to understand.


14. Idempotency
    An operation is idempotent if making the same request multiple times has the same intended effect as making it once.
    Examples under HTTP semantics:
        GET    → idempotent
        PUT    → idempotent
        DELETE → idempotent

    POST is generally not idempotent.
    Example:
        POST /orders
            Sending it twice could create:
                Order #101
                Order #102


15. Idempotency keys
    For critical operations such as payments/orders, you may use:
        Idempotency-Key: abc123

    Client sends:
        POST /payments
        Idempotency-Key: abc123

    If the request is accidentally retried, the server can recognize the same operation rather than processing it as a new payment.
    Conceptually:
        Request
        ↓
        Idempotency Key
        ↓
        Already processed?
        ┌───────┴───────┐
        Yes             No
        ↓                ↓
        Return result   Process

    This is a very good real-world interview topic.


16. Request validation
    Never trust:
        req.body
        req.params
        req.query
    Validate them.

    Example using a validation library:
        const userSchema = {
            name: "required",
            email: "valid email"
        };

    In real applications, libraries such as:
        Zod
        Joi
        Yup
        express-validator
    can be used.

    Validation should happen before business logic.
        Request
        ↓
        Validation
        ↓
        Authentication
        ↓
        Authorization
        ↓
        Controller
        ↓
        Service
        ↓
        Database

    Exact middleware ordering depends on your requirements, but the principle is to reject invalid/untrusted input early.


17. Consistent response structure
    Avoid random response shapes:
        {
            "name": "John"
        }

    then another endpoint:
        {
            "result": {
                "name": "Alex"
            }
        }

    A consistent API contract is easier for frontend/client developers.
    For example:
        {
            "data": {
                "id": 123,
                "name": "John"
            }
        }

    Error:
        {
            "error": {
                "code": "USER_NOT_FOUND",
                "message": "User not found"
            }
        }

    The exact structure is a team/API design decision; consistency matters more than one universal format.


18. REST is stateless
    This is one of the fundamental REST principles.
    Each request should contain the information necessary for the server to process it.

    For example:
        GET /profile
            Authorization: Bearer <token>

    The server shouldn't depend on hidden state from a previous request to understand this request.

    Interview answer
        "Statelessness means each request contains the context required to process it. The server doesn't rely on application session state from previous requests as part of the REST interaction."

    Note: authentication systems can still use server-side session storage; "stateless REST" is a broader architectural constraint, not a claim that every possible authentication implementation must be token-based.


19. Common REST interview scenario
    Interviewer:
        "Design APIs for an e-commerce application."

    A reasonable starting point:
        Users
            GET    /api/v1/users
            GET    /api/v1/users/:id
            POST   /api/v1/users
            PATCH  /api/v1/users/:id
            DELETE /api/v1/users/:id

        Products
            GET    /api/v1/products
            GET    /api/v1/products/:id
            POST   /api/v1/products
            PATCH  /api/v1/products/:id
            DELETE /api/v1/products/:id

        Orders
            GET    /api/v1/orders
            GET    /api/v1/orders/:id
            POST   /api/v1/orders
            PATCH  /api/v1/orders/:id

    Then discuss:
        Authentication
        Authorization
        Validation
        Pagination
        Filtering
        Error handling
        Idempotency
        Rate limiting
        Caching
        Database transactions

    That's how you move from "I know REST" to "I can design APIs."


20. Top interview questions
    Q: PUT vs PATCH?
        PUT generally replaces the resource representation; PATCH partially modifies it.

    Q: 401 vs 403?
        401 → authentication missing/invalid.
        403 → authenticated but not authorized.

    Q: 200 vs 201?
        200 → successful request.
        201 → resource successfully created.

    Q: 204?
        Success with no response body.

    Q: What is idempotency?
        Repeated identical requests produce the same intended resource state/effect.

    Q: Why pagination?
        To avoid returning huge datasets and reduce memory, database, network, and response costs.

    Q: Offset vs cursor pagination?
        Offset is simpler; cursor generally scales better for large/changing datasets.

    Q: What does stateless mean?
        Each request carries the information required to process it without depending on previous request context stored as part of the REST interaction.

    Q: How do you design a good REST API?
        Use resource-oriented URLs, appropriate HTTP methods/status codes, consistent response structures, validation, authentication/authorization, pagination, filtering, versioning where needed, and proper error handling.




